WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.233
[Interviewer:] Do you happen to have diabetes?

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:04.366
[Woman 1:] No I don't.

00:00:04.366 --> 00:00:07.460
At least I don't know if I do.
[Interviewer:] Well...

00:00:07.466 --> 00:00:10.399
[Woman 1:] I get checkups every year, but not for 
diabetes.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.566
[Man 1:] I don't feel like I got it. 
I never had any problems with my health.

00:00:14.566 --> 00:00:16.632
[Interviewer:] Well, have you had a checkup 
recently?

00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:21.033
[Man 1:] A year ago, x-rays. 
From one of these mobile units.

00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:22.666
[Interviewer:] What is diabetes?

00:00:22.666 --> 00:00:30.266
[Man 2:] It's when um you don't have enough of...
that chemical that dissolves your blood...

00:00:30.266 --> 00:00:35.699
dissolves your sugar...you don't have enough of it 
um, I can't think of the name of it right now.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:39.333
[Woman 2:] It's a disease a, I don't know, 
you've got too much sugar

00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:40.333
or you don't have enough, something in your 
blood.

00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:43.699
[Interviewer:] You have too much or not enough?
[Woman 2:] Something like that...no...

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.266
yeah too much that's what it is.

00:00:45.266 --> 00:00:47.432
[Man 3:] I don't think it's sugar in your blood, 
it's sugar in your pancreas.

00:00:47.433 --> 00:00:50.633
[Interviewer:] Do you know what diabetes is?
[Woman 3:] Yeah.

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:52.366
[Interviewer:] What is it?

00:00:52.366 --> 00:00:56.232
[Woman 3:] Well, it's an accumulation of sugar in 
the system.

00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:58.033
[Interviewer:] What are the symptoms?

00:00:58.033 --> 00:01:10.899
[Woman 3:] Well, you're thirsty I know,
and I believe loss of weight, and nervousness.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.733
[Interviewer:] Have you ever had it?
[Woman 3:] No, I never had it.

00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:23.099
[Woman 4:] Well, the symptoms is you go into a 
coma, you can go into a coma,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.900
or you have sugar in your urine, you have it in 
your blood.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.366
[Interviewer:] How would you know if you have 
diabetes?

00:01:29.366 --> 00:01:33.766
[Woman 4:] I don't know. Many times I think I have 
it myself.

00:01:33.766 --> 00:01:37.566
[Narrator:] Well, maybe she does have it, 
anyone can get it.

00:01:37.566 --> 00:01:44.332
But three out of four diabetics are over 40 and 
overweight, and the majority are women.

00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:50.466
All the people interviewed know something about 
diabetes, but none of them had a very clear idea.

00:01:50.466 --> 00:01:54.266
They were aware that it has something to do with 
too much sugar in the blood

00:01:54.266 --> 00:01:57.699
or in the urine, or somewhere.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.700
Before we go further, let me explain.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:06.300
Diabetes is too much sugar in your blood, 
and some of this overflows into the urine.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.900
What causes this?

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:13.633
Not having enough or the right amount of insulin,
which comes from the pancreas.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.599
How does this affect you?

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.466
Well, let me introduce you to some people who 
have diabetes.

00:02:21.466 --> 00:02:24.999
Mr. Mundt, who is a mechanic.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.900
Mrs. Cook, a club woman.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.866
Mrs. Jessick, a grandmother.

00:02:30.866 --> 00:02:33.966
Kathy Nicannon, a young girl.

00:02:33.966 --> 00:02:37.066
Mr. Altock, a retired baker,

00:02:37.066 --> 00:02:40.899
and Mrs. Levine, a housewife.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:46.766
Some are old, and one at least is quite young, 
but whether old or young,

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:48.999
diabetes hasn't dampened their spirits.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.566
This isn't surprising among those who know they 
have diabetes.

00:02:53.566 --> 00:02:58.666
For them, it's a matter of taking their treatment 
and keeping the disease under control.

00:02:58.666 --> 00:03:03.766
It's the ones who have diabetes and don't know it 
who are likely to be dispirited.

00:03:03.766 --> 00:03:08.866
In fact, one of the early signs of diabetes is having 
no energy.

00:03:08.866 --> 00:03:12.299
However, there are more obvious symptoms than 
that.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:15.420
Let's hear from the people you just met 
and find out

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.500
how they first discovered they had diabetes.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.760
Take Mrs. Levine, for example.

00:03:22.766 --> 00:03:25.566
She has a family history of diabetes.

00:03:25.566 --> 00:03:29.566
In fact, this is true of one out of every three 
diabetics.

00:03:29.566 --> 00:03:32.432
[Mrs. Levine:] I had been told by the doctors that 
the possibility of me

00:03:32.433 --> 00:03:34.966
becoming a diabetic was very strong.

00:03:34.966 --> 00:03:40.466
Both my parents are diabetics and I've lived with it 
most of my life.

00:03:40.466 --> 00:03:45.632
[Narrator:] Mrs. Levine had an early warning 
when routine tests during her pregnancies

00:03:45.633 --> 00:03:48.899
indicated a tendency toward diabetes.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:55.433
But it wasn't until some time after her children were 
born--fine normal babies but rather big--

00:03:55.433 --> 00:03:58.733
that the three main symptoms thrust themselves 
upon her.

00:03:58.733 --> 00:04:02.966
Thirst, excessive urination, and hunger.

00:04:02.966 --> 00:04:07.140
The family tendency to have diabetes asserted 
itself,

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.833
and Mrs. Levine was diagnosed as a diabetic.

00:04:10.833 --> 00:04:15.066
On the other hand, some people have no warning 
at all.

00:04:15.066 --> 00:04:21.332
[Mr. Mundt:] I was driving along and all of a sudden 
I felt faint, so I moved over to the curb.

00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:24.733
I blacked out.

00:04:24.733 --> 00:04:29.666
I just got unconscious, and then I went back to the 
doctor.

00:04:29.666 --> 00:04:31.666
I came to the doctor, he examined me and...

00:04:31.666 --> 00:04:36.166
[Narrator:] At first Mr. Mundt had what appeared to 
be a fainting spell.

00:04:36.166 --> 00:04:40.366
It wasn't until his second attack that the diagnosis 
was made.

00:04:40.366 --> 00:04:46.232
[Mr. Mundt:] About six months later I, all of a 
sudden I felt dizzy.

00:04:46.233 --> 00:04:53.833
I pulled over to curb, and I stood there for a minute  or two, or a couple of minutes, and I came back to it,

00:04:53.833 --> 00:05:00.599
and they sent me down to take a blood test 
and a urine test,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:03.533
and I found out that I had diabetes.

00:05:03.533 --> 00:05:11.199
[Narrator:] Other symptoms of the disease are 
infections that will not heal, crops of boils, itching.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:13.900
In Mrs. Jessick's case it was boils.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:20.533
[Mrs. Jessick:] And thereafter I had about 30 boils, 
so I went to see the doctor about it,

00:05:20.533 --> 00:05:28.733
and the doctor sent me to a cardiologist for a test,
and he discovered that I had diabetes,

00:05:28.733 --> 00:05:32.666
not much, but diabetes nevertheless.

00:05:32.666 --> 00:05:37.766
[Interviewer:] Though most diabetics are middle- aged or older when they find they have it,

00:05:37.766 --> 00:05:44.966
this was not so with Kathy Nicannon. Kathy is one of quite a large family, and is the only diabetic among them.

00:05:44.966 --> 00:05:47.132
She is now 13.

00:05:47.133 --> 00:05:50.366
[Kathy:] I've had diabetes for two years, since I was 
11.

00:05:50.366 --> 00:05:55.099
I had a large infection on my leg and my mother 
decided since it wasn't healing,

00:05:55.100 --> 00:06:00.366
we should go to the doctor and have him check it,
and she was also worried about symptoms I had

00:06:00.366 --> 00:06:04.332
because I was urinating frequently and drinking a 
lot of water.

00:06:04.333 --> 00:06:08.933
[Interviewer:] The doctor took a urine test and the 
slow-healing infection proved to be

00:06:08.933 --> 00:06:11.433
a symptom of diabetes.

00:06:11.433 --> 00:06:14.233
The same thing happened in Mr. Altock's case.

00:06:14.233 --> 00:06:16.633
Another accidental discovery.

00:06:16.633 --> 00:06:23.899
[Mr. Altock:] I was taking a box down from the rafts 
when the box slipped and hit me right in the eye,

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:28.966
right here above the eye it cut the skin.

00:06:28.966 --> 00:06:37.332
Then I was going to my doctor see, when he 
couldn't find out why this didn't heal that cut, see,

00:06:37.333 --> 00:06:40.633
and then he said there's something wrong with 
you.

00:06:40.633 --> 00:06:46.499
All right, then he took a test and then he found 
sugar in my urine.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.266
[Interviewer:] Finally there was Mrs. Cook,
who noticed no symptoms and came very close

00:06:51.266 --> 00:06:56.799
to dying before she found out she had diabetes, 
but let her tell you about it.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.000
[Mrs. Cook:] I went into a serious state of shock.

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:08.900
Nobody will tell me what happened, but I know that 
it was serious because there were about four

00:07:08.900 --> 00:07:18.600
four nurses and I don't know what about my bed,
and they started intravenous stomach,

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:24.600
I forget what you call it but it was a stomach pump 
anyhow, and intravenous feeding, and I know that

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:30.833
they brought the priest into me and gave me the 
last rites.

00:07:30.833 --> 00:07:33.799
I was conscious a moment or two and then I'd fade out.

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:39.233
But I was in that state for three days.

00:07:39.233 --> 00:07:44.199
[Interviewer:] Fortunately for Mrs. Cook, she 
received excellent medical care immediately.

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:48.200
Had she been alone when this happened, she 
might have died.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:51.800
You have met six people out of the million and a 
half Americans

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.933
who are under treatment for diabetes.

00:07:53.933 --> 00:07:55.699
Known diabetics.

00:07:55.700 --> 00:08:00.933
It is estimated that another million and a half have 
diabetes and don't know it.

00:08:00.933 --> 00:08:04.133
These are the diabetics unknown.

00:08:04.133 --> 00:08:09.533
They range from the mildly afflicted, who have no 
other symptoms than tiring easily,

00:08:09.533 --> 00:08:15.133
to those who have persistent signs of thirst, 
excessive urination, and hunger,

00:08:15.133 --> 00:08:19.633
to those who will only find out that they have 
diabetes by going into a coma,

00:08:19.633 --> 00:08:23.699
in which case they may not live to find out.

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:29.033
Just how these signs and symptoms relate to what 
is happening inside the human body is something

00:08:29.033 --> 00:08:35.766
that you should understand, so we are going to 
show you some animation.

00:08:35.766 --> 00:08:42.232
In the normal person, sugar that has been eaten 
goes from the intestine into the liver.

00:08:42.233 --> 00:08:48.633
Some sugar is kept by the liver and the rest makes 
its way into the bloodstream by way of the heart.

00:08:48.633 --> 00:08:54.133
Some sugar enters the muscle cells, where it is 
burned at once to provide energy.

00:08:54.133 --> 00:08:57.733
Some is converted into fat and stored for later use.

00:08:57.733 --> 00:09:04.333
What is left over travels to the kidney and back 
again into the blood system.

00:09:04.333 --> 00:09:09.533
Insulin is a chemical produced by the islets of 
Langerhans in the pancreas.

00:09:09.533 --> 00:09:14.133
Its function is to make it possible for sugar to 
penetrate the cells,

00:09:14.133 --> 00:09:17.333
particularly the muscle and fat cells.

00:09:17.333 --> 00:09:22.366
In the normal person, the flow of insulin decreases 
if smaller amounts of sugar are taken

00:09:22.366 --> 00:09:26.366
into the system, and increases with more sugar.

00:09:26.366 --> 00:09:31.732
Just the right amount for the occasion is fed into 
the blood system to enable the sugar

00:09:31.733 --> 00:09:35.266
to enter the muscle, liver, and fat cells.

00:09:35.266 --> 00:09:40.199
The diabetic's chemical problem is that he does 
not have a natural flow of insulin.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:43.166
It is either deficient or nonexistent.

00:09:43.166 --> 00:09:47.520
Since the sugar can't get into the cells, it remains 
in the bloodstream,

00:09:47.620 --> 00:09:50.960
so the diabetic has high blood sugar.

00:09:50.966 --> 00:09:55.840
Since the blood reaching the kidney has too much 
sugar, some passes into the urine,

00:09:55.860 --> 00:10:00.820
so sugar in the urine is another indication of 
diabetes.

00:10:00.833 --> 00:10:04.666
The diabetic drinks a great deal of water 
to enable the kidneys

00:10:04.666 --> 00:10:07.299
to get rid of the excess sugar.

00:10:07.300 --> 00:10:12.200
This water passing through the body rapidly 
results in frequent urination.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:17.400
Without insulin, the cells cannot burn sugar for 
energy, and so the body begins

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:20.533
to burn stored fat instead.

00:10:20.533 --> 00:10:27.433
Similarly, the fat cells cannot take in more sugar to 
replenish their supplies of fat.

00:10:27.433 --> 00:10:33.133
As the store of body fat is used up, the diabetic 
yearns for more food,

00:10:33.133 --> 00:10:36.299
trying in vain to replenish the fat cells.

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:41.766
Instead he usually gets thinner. It's a vicious circle.

00:10:41.766 --> 00:10:46.600
One great danger of diabetes is that the burning of 
these abnormal quantities of fat

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:54.160
may produce excess acidity in the body, acidosis,
which induces coma and even death.

00:10:54.166 --> 00:11:00.666
This is why until quite recently, diabetes was a 
terrifying word.

00:11:00.666 --> 00:11:06.466
Today, except among diabetics unknown, acidosis 
rarely occurs.

00:11:06.466 --> 00:11:12.466
The remarkable discovery of insulin in 1921,
and the recent development of new medicines

00:11:12.466 --> 00:11:18.799
that can be taken in tablet form, makes it possible 
for diabetics to live normal, happy lives.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:24.966
Actually, some diabetics keep the disease under 
control without any medication whatsoever.

00:11:24.966 --> 00:11:30.866
However, all diabetics have this in common: they 
must watch their diets

00:11:30.866 --> 00:11:33.466
and take the right amount of exercise.

00:11:33.466 --> 00:11:40.066
In fact, for the diabetic, medication, diet, and 
exercise are interrelated.

00:11:40.066 --> 00:11:45.632
Keeping the balance between these three is what 
every diabetic must learn to do.

00:11:45.633 --> 00:11:49.466
In the beginning, it's hard, as Kathy's mother found 
out.

00:11:49.466 --> 00:11:51.766
[Kathy's mother:] She was used to eating her lunch 
at 11.

00:11:51.766 --> 00:11:56.832
We had been shopping, the excitement of her 
shopping and trying on clothes and so forth,

00:11:56.833 --> 00:12:03.033
and at 12 o'clock I heard the whistle and I said 
gee, we better get her her lunch.

00:12:03.033 --> 00:12:10.366
As we went into the ice cream parlor, she said to 
me, "I don't feel well," and she was breaking out

00:12:10.366 --> 00:12:17.499
into a sweat, which is the fact that she had more 
insulin than she had sugar.

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:22.866
So I immediately asked for a glass of orange juice 
which they gave me very quickly.

00:12:22.866 --> 00:12:25.199
[Narrator:] Kathy felt better right away.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.266
Like all diabetics should, she carries a card that 
identifies her as a diabetic

00:12:30.266 --> 00:12:31.932
in case of an emergency.

00:12:31.933 --> 00:12:37.266
However, now that she has learned to balance her 
diet and insulin against her activities,

00:12:37.266 --> 00:12:40.366
there is little likelihood that she will have any 
trouble.

00:12:40.366 --> 00:12:46.099
When she goes bowling or to dances, she knows 
she can eat more and she does.

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:50.433
Experience has shown her how to adjust almost 
without thinking about it.

00:12:50.433 --> 00:12:55.266
[Kathy:] They usually have cookies and I eat some 
of them, because I'm exercising, dancing.

00:12:55.266 --> 00:13:00.266
[Narrator:] It's up to the doctor to decide which, if 
any, medication the patient should have,

00:13:00.266 --> 00:13:01.699
and how much of it.

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:07.233
For severe cases of diabetes, injections of insulin 
are needed to move the sugar out of the blood

00:13:07.233 --> 00:13:10.399
and into the muscle and fat cells where it belongs.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:15.566
For others, the new pills may be taken to help the 
pancreas secrete enough insulin.

00:13:15.566 --> 00:13:18.799
In many others, it's simply a matter of diet.

00:13:18.800 --> 00:13:25.560
[Kathy:] I was put on insulin, gradually increased 'til 
it came to the right amount,

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:29.160
and urine tests and once in a while blood tests.

00:13:29.166 --> 00:13:31.232
I administer the injections myself.

00:13:31.233 --> 00:13:36.699
[Narrator:] Self-discipline is a vital ingredient 
of treatment when it comes to diabetes.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:40.233
Mrs. Levine is a good example of the ideal patient.

00:13:40.233 --> 00:13:45.299
[Mrs. Levine:] As a diabetic, there are three things I 
have to be concerned about:

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:51.600
that I take my medicine daily, that I watch my diet 
very carefully,

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:55.566
and that I do my exercise daily.

00:13:55.566 --> 00:14:00.832
[Narrator:] Every few months Mrs. Levine sees her 
doctor. He gives her a blood test,

00:14:00.833 --> 00:14:04.399
checks her weight and reviews her diet chart.

00:14:04.400 --> 00:14:09.000
Mrs. Levine finds it's much easier to handle the 
dietary requirements

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:14.666
then when her mother and father had diabetes.
Everything then had to be weighed and measured.

00:14:14.666 --> 00:14:19.732
[Mrs. Levine:] I do most of my shopping at the 
supermarket and I buy

00:14:19.733 --> 00:14:21.566
very much what anybody else buys.

00:14:21.566 --> 00:14:24.999
I buy a lot of fruit at the fruit department.

00:14:25.000 --> 00:14:30.200
[Narrator:] Mr. Altock takes medicine and watches 
his diet but nostalgically.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:34.700
[Mr. Altock:] You know I used to like noodles and 
pancakes and applesauce and all this stuff.

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:37.533
I could eat that for breakfast, you know, in the 
morning.

00:14:37.533 --> 00:14:42.099
He cut that all out and uh, put me on a strict diet.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:43.900
Vegetables, less bread.

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:53.600
I eat two slices of bread in the morning for 
breakfast, and then I eat a, I eat grapefruit

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:56.900
without the sugar, the way they come, no sugar.

00:14:56.900 --> 00:15:03.566
I eat oatmeal, I put salt on there instead of sugar 
and then I use only skim milk because the doctor,

00:15:03.566 --> 00:15:10.366
at that time of the examination, when they put me on them pills, no fat, no butter,

00:15:10.366 --> 00:15:13.166
he cut me out of the butter, he cut me out of the 
fat.

00:15:13.166 --> 00:15:17.066
[Narrator:] Mrs. Cook takes her medicine and stays 
on her diet most of the time.

00:15:17.066 --> 00:15:27.999
[Mrs. Cook:] I need no injections, and I take two 
tablets a day and that's really all that...

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.800
all the inconvenience.

00:15:29.800 --> 00:15:36.066
There are times that perhaps I don't have quite as 
much energy, but generally that's because

00:15:36.066 --> 00:15:38.499
I haven't lived up to the rules.

00:15:38.500 --> 00:15:42.566
[Narrator:] Mr. Mundt is one of the diabetics who 
needs neither insulin nor pills.

00:15:42.566 --> 00:15:46.699
He keeps the balance entirely through diet 
and his strenuous work provides

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:48.800
most of his daily exercise.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.933
[Mr. Mundt:] I repair radiators, and recoil the 
radiators.

00:15:51.933 --> 00:16:00.699
This is a small radiator, but some of them are very, 
very big, the very big radiators, you know,

00:16:00.700 --> 00:16:06.733
require some strenuous handling, lifting up, and all 
that.

00:16:06.733 --> 00:16:10.399
[Narrator:] Mr. Mundt, like most diabetics, can hold 
a full-time job.

00:16:10.400 --> 00:16:17.233
In fact, three out of four diabetics are not restricted 
in any daily activity.

00:16:17.233 --> 00:16:22.033
The new medicines and greater knowledge of the 
disease have made all this possible.

00:16:22.033 --> 00:16:24.033
Years have been added to the diabetic's life.

00:16:24.033 --> 00:16:28.866
In fact, some diabetics are living much longer 
than a great many other people

00:16:28.866 --> 00:16:32.332
who don't take such good care of themselves.

00:16:32.333 --> 00:16:37.266
But what about the million and a half persons who 
have diabetes and don't know it?

00:16:37.266 --> 00:16:42.266
The diabetics unknown. Why don't these people 
know?

00:16:42.266 --> 00:16:49.132
Many do not recognize or do not have the common 
symptoms of thirst, excessive urination,

00:16:49.133 --> 00:16:52.099
incessant hunger.

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:56.000
Others don't want to face up to the fact.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:01.200
Some find it out when they go their doctors for 
other complaints.

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:06.166
Far too few make the discovery in the best 
possible way, the annual medical checkup

00:17:06.166 --> 00:17:09.166
that looks for any and all diseases.

00:17:09.166 --> 00:17:14.032
Mrs. Cook, whom you will remember almost died 
because she didn't know she had diabetes,

00:17:14.033 --> 00:17:16.599
has become an eloquent spokesman for this.

00:17:16.600 --> 00:17:24.133
[Mrs. Cook:] I hope the time is coming when people 
will go for, more people will go for their

00:17:24.133 --> 00:17:33.833
annual checkup, and let the checkup include a, uh, 
testing for diabetes.

00:17:33.833 --> 00:17:38.466
[Narrator:] Diabetics are also found through 
screening programs organized by voluntary health

00:17:38.466 --> 00:17:44.966
associations with the help of the county medical 
societies, and by local public health departments.

00:17:44.966 --> 00:17:51.132
Sometimes they are held in local hospitals. Such a 
place is the Middlesex Hospital

00:17:51.133 --> 00:17:56.199
in New Brunswick, New Jersey, where Mrs. Susan 
Ridenaur went for her test.

00:17:56.200 --> 00:18:02.266
Mrs. Ridenaur first became interested in diabetes 
through the American Diabetes Association.

00:18:02.266 --> 00:18:06.266
She read a pamphlet and discovered that women 
of her age group were most likely

00:18:06.266 --> 00:18:14.632
candidates for the disease.

00:18:14.633 --> 00:18:21.566
There was a quite a group the morning she went to 
the hospital, and she stood in line to register.

00:18:21.566 --> 00:18:26.999
In the laboratory the doctor took a sample of her 
blood.

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:31.366
The technician prepared it for the automatic 
machine that makes it possible to screen many

00:18:31.366 --> 00:18:37.899
people quickly, by handling 12 samples at a time, 
or up to 1,000 a day.

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:40.866
It takes five minutes for the processing.

00:18:40.866 --> 00:18:45.232
One test tube comes off the machine every 30 
seconds.

00:18:45.233 --> 00:18:48.166
Mrs. Ridenaur waited for her results.

00:18:48.166 --> 00:18:58.766
[Sound of test tubes being shaken]

00:18:58.766 --> 00:19:02.266
The test tube dropped off the rack. It was 
colorless.

00:19:02.266 --> 00:19:07.332
This meant the Mrs. Ridenaur was a suspicious 
case.

00:19:07.333 --> 00:19:13.866
She was referred to her doctor for a further 
checkup, and he in turn was notified of the results.

00:19:13.866 --> 00:19:18.332
Mrs. Ridenaur's doctor told her that he would have 
to do more-definitive tests to find out

00:19:18.333 --> 00:19:21.499
whether she actually had diabetes.

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:28.100
That this was merely an indication; however, it 
would be well to do these tests right away.

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:33.500
If people could only know how much more 
uncomfortable, not to mention the danger aspect,

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:37.600
it is to live with untreated diabetes, than it is to take 
care of it.

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:44.333
I'm sure there would not be a million and a half 
undetected diabetics in the country today.

00:19:44.333 --> 00:19:49.233
If you need more convincing on this point,
let's ask our diabetics what difference it has made

00:19:49.233 --> 00:19:50.933
in the way they live.

00:19:50.933 --> 00:19:54.966
[Mrs. Levine:] You know they say when you have 
an incurable disease, you live a longer

00:19:54.966 --> 00:20:00.732
and healthier life, and it's very true because you're 
going to think of everything you do

00:20:00.733 --> 00:20:02.433
before you do it.

00:20:02.433 --> 00:20:06.399
[Kathy's mother:] Kathy is an honor student, a high 
honor student in the enriched course,

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:10.100
so in other words she's in the eighth grade but 
doing ninth grade work.

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:16.333
The one thing that was stressed was that 
you do not treat the diabetic any differently.

00:20:16.333 --> 00:20:18.633
We do not treat Kathy any differently.

00:20:18.633 --> 00:20:23.099
And of course Kathy is of the nature that she 
wouldn't allow herself

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:24.833
to be treated any differently anyway.

00:20:24.833 --> 00:20:30.533
She has never felt sorry for herself, she has no 
reason to feel sorry for herself.

00:20:30.533 --> 00:20:35.033
[Mr. Altock:] I do a whole lot of work around the 
house.

00:20:35.033 --> 00:20:39.133
But what has to be done here in the house, I paint.

00:20:39.133 --> 00:20:46.899
I shoveled snow this winter, course snow shovel is 
not... the doctor tells you if you're over 65

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:51.733
and I'm going on 72, you shouldn't shovel so much 
snow.

00:20:51.733 --> 00:20:57.766
[Narrator:] Note that it is Mr. Altock's age 
and not his diabetes that is the restriction here.

00:20:57.766 --> 00:21:00.532
Mrs. Jessick is more philosophical.

00:21:00.533 --> 00:21:06.966
[Mrs. Jessick:] Well at first I was very unhappy, 
but then I realized it's a problem,

00:21:06.966 --> 00:21:09.566
you'll have to adjust yourself.

00:21:09.566 --> 00:21:14.499
[Narrator:] Once the pattern has been established, 
diabetes is not hard to live with.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:19.800
[Mrs. Jessick:] It's a lot of things in life I had to get 
used to, so I had to get used to that, too.

00:21:19.800 --> 00:21:23.833
[Narrator:] And so these people and the other 
known diabetics throughout the country

00:21:23.833 --> 00:21:30.166
are safe, happy, feel fine, and live long and useful 
lives.

00:21:30.166 --> 00:21:36.099
Two generations ago diabetes was a threat to life 
itself, but not today.

00:21:36.100 --> 00:21:40.660
The only real threat is not finding out in time  by having annual checkups,

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:47.820
or ignoring the hereditary factor, and such common symptoms  as excessive thirst, urination, hunger,

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:55.733
boils and the rest, and becoming by default a diabetic unknown.

00:21:55.733 --> 00:21:56.999
[Interviewer on street:] Do you happen to have 
diabetes?

00:21:57.000 --> 00:21:59.733
[Woman 5:] No, I don't believe so.
[Interviewer:] Anybody in your family?

00:21:59.733 --> 00:22:03.933
[Woman 5:] No. Well, my husband I think had it 
slightly, but he's deceased now.

00:22:03.933 --> 00:22:04.966
[Interviewer:] What does he do for it?

00:22:04.966 --> 00:22:09.066
[Woman 5:] Well, he doesn't do anything now, he's 
dead.

00:22:09.066 --> 00:22:14.832
[Woman 6:] Well, I have a little, I did have it, I did 
have diabetes.

00:22:14.833 --> 00:22:15.666
[Interviewer:] What did you do for it?

00:22:15.666 --> 00:22:21.332
[Woman 6:] Well, I went to Meadowbrook Hospital 
and I had some insulation and I feel better now.

00:22:21.333 --> 00:22:26.333
[Interviewer:] Does it incapacitate a person?

00:22:26.333 --> 00:22:31.666
[Woman 6:] No...they take insulin shots everyday 
and can keep normal lives like the rest of us,

00:22:31.666 --> 00:22:33.399
if they take care of themselves.

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:37.466
[Interviewer:] All right, thank you very much.

00:22:40.966 --> 00:22:42.432
[Director:] Action.

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:46.840
[Joan Wolle:] I'm Joan Wolle, State Director of 
Health Education in Maryland,

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:51.260
and also a representative of the Conference of 
State Health Educators.

00:22:51.266 --> 00:22:56.599
I'm here to speak for you, the audience, and I hope 
I'll ask some of the questions

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:59.066
for which you'd like the answers.

00:22:59.066 --> 00:23:04.899
Here to discuss some of the aspects of diabetes 
are three physicians who are specialists

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:14.000
in the field: Dr. Arthur Krosnick, Dr. Francis 
Lukens, and Dr. Kare Gundersen.

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:18.366
Let's review the usual symptoms of diabetes, Dr. 
Gundersen.

00:23:18.366 --> 00:23:27.066
[Dr. Gunderson:] The symptoms are fatigue, weight 
loss, excessive thirst and urination, hunger,

00:23:27.066 --> 00:23:35.232
cramps in the legs, boils that won't heal, or wounds 
that won't heal.

00:23:35.233 --> 00:23:40.733
[Joan Wolle:] Statistics show that diabetes is most 
likely to occur to those who have relatives

00:23:40.733 --> 00:23:42.233
who have the disease.

00:23:42.233 --> 00:23:45.933
Would you comment on this hereditary factor, Dr. 
Lukens?

00:23:45.933 --> 00:23:52.133
[Dr. Lukens:] Diabetes is six to eight times as 
frequent in the family of a diabetic as it is

00:23:52.133 --> 00:23:54.266
in the general population.

00:23:54.266 --> 00:23:59.166
We know little about human heredity,
but this at least gives us a practical guide

00:23:59.166 --> 00:24:02.166
in the early detection of this condition.

00:24:02.166 --> 00:24:07.266
[Joan Wolle:] If a person has diabetes in his family 
and he enjoys eating sweets,

00:24:07.266 --> 00:24:08.866
should he stop eating sweets?

00:24:08.866 --> 00:24:13.366
[Dr. Lukens:] The sweets only matter as part of the 
total food he eats.

00:24:13.366 --> 00:24:17.599
He should keep his weight right on whatever diet 
will do this.

00:24:17.600 --> 00:24:21.933
[Joan Wolle:] Dr. Krosnick, who is most likely to 
have diabetes?

00:24:21.933 --> 00:24:26.733
[Dr. Krosnick:] Ms. Wolle, women have a higher 
chance of developing diabetes than men,

00:24:26.733 --> 00:24:30.333
especially those who have had several 
pregnancies.

00:24:30.333 --> 00:24:36.933
Overweight may be one of the causative factors in 
diabetes and we know that the most common age

00:24:36.933 --> 00:24:42.333
is that between 40 and 70 when diabetes first appears.

00:24:42.333 --> 00:24:46.133
[Joan Wolle:] Isn't it true that an early sign of 
diabetes for some people

00:24:46.133 --> 00:24:48.333
is actually loss of weight?

00:24:48.333 --> 00:24:54.833
[Dr. Krosnick:] Yes, some diabetics have an 
enormous appetite and eat huge quantities of food

00:24:54.833 --> 00:24:57.933
and lose weight in spite of this.

00:24:57.933 --> 00:25:03.099
[Joan Wolle:] One thing that struck me about the 
film was that Mrs. Cook and Mr. Mundt didn't seem

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:06.833
to have any symptoms of the disease before it was 
diagnosed.

00:25:06.833 --> 00:25:09.366
Does this often happen?

00:25:09.366 --> 00:25:14.766
[Dr. Krosnick:] Well, mild diabetics may have no 
symptoms at all except fatigue.

00:25:14.766 --> 00:25:22.099
This is most likely to be true of those patients who 
are detected by an annual checkup examination.

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:27.633
[Joan Wolle:] I guess this means that the only truly 
preventive measure that a person can take is to

00:25:27.633 --> 00:25:30.699
have an annual physical checkup.

00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:33.166
[Dr. Gunderson:] Yes, that's quite true.

00:25:33.166 --> 00:25:38.666
It's a good idea to see the doctor once a year, 
maybe on one's birthday, and be checked out

00:25:38.666 --> 00:25:44.632
completely with an examination of urine and blood,
and of course then you could also

00:25:44.633 --> 00:25:46.466
check for other diseases at the same time.

00:25:46.466 --> 00:25:51.199
[Joan Wolle:] Dr. Lukens, isn't it true that quite a 
few county medical societies have sponsored

00:25:51.200 --> 00:25:53.033
diabetes detection programs?

00:25:53.033 --> 00:26:00.033
[Dr. Lukens:] Yes, the Philadelphia County Medical 
Society joined in testing 3,500 people last fall.

00:26:00.033 --> 00:26:03.966
[Joan Wolle:] Dr. Krosnick, what are some of the 
testing methods?

00:26:03.966 --> 00:26:09.066
[Dr. Krosnick:] Well, we use urine tests and blood 
examinations in New Jersey.

00:26:09.066 --> 00:26:13.532
We find that urine tests are rapid and inexpensive,

00:26:13.533 --> 00:26:15.999
but blood tests are much more accurate.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:22.220
In some detection programs, automatic analysis 
and techniques of this type

00:26:22.260 --> 00:26:26.133
will provide an answer in just a few moments.

00:26:26.133 --> 00:26:30.999
Most of the people tested do not have diabetes, 
but those who are suspected are sent

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:33.700
to their family doctors for further tests.

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:38.300
[Joan Wolle:] You know sometimes the person who 
is told he has diabetes is very frightened.

00:26:38.300 --> 00:26:44.566
[Dr. Gunderson:] Yes, that's true, being a diabetic 
myself I can understand this feeling.

00:26:44.566 --> 00:26:48.366
However, once I was told about the disease and 
learned how to take care of it,

00:26:48.366 --> 00:26:50.199
the fear disappeared.

00:26:50.200 --> 00:26:55.966
I've taken insulin myself now for 22 years 
and would rather take a shot than shave,

00:26:55.966 --> 00:26:58.199
since it takes less time and effort.

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:01.300
And of course, most diabetics don't have to take 
insulin.

00:27:01.300 --> 00:27:05.500
They can get along quite well on pills or even on 
diet alone.

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:11.933
[Joan Wolle:] Dr. Lukens, isn't it true that diabetics 
should try to avoid cuts and other injuries?

00:27:11.933 --> 00:27:16.399
[Dr. Lukens:] Most diabetics get over cuts very 
well.

00:27:16.400 --> 00:27:22.133
The important exception is injury to the feet, 
particularly in elderly people.

00:27:22.133 --> 00:27:24.166
[Joan Wolle:] Is there a cure for diabetes?

00:27:24.166 --> 00:27:30.100
[Dr. Lukens:] No, there is no cure for diabetes, but it can be completely controlled.

00:27:30.280 --> 00:27:37.233
Just as the near-sighted person can have normal vision with glasses without actually curing the eyes,

00:27:37.233 --> 00:27:42.799
so the diabetic can lead a completely normal and useful life if he takes care of the condition.

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:46.266
[Joan Wolle:] Some people believe that diabetics 
don't have normal children.

00:27:46.266 --> 00:27:48.666
Isn't this an old wives' tale?

00:27:48.666 --> 00:27:55.366
[Dr. Gunderson:] Yes, diabetics have normal 
children, I have four very normal ones myself.

00:27:55.366 --> 00:27:59.732
[Joan Wolle:] All this seems to add up to the important point that diabetes isn't too bad

00:27:59.733 --> 00:28:06.133
a disease to have, that is, if you realize that you have it and you take care of it properly.

00:28:06.133 --> 00:28:11.333
[Dr. Gunderson:] Diabetes hasn't interfered with 
my life to any appreciable extent.

00:28:11.333 --> 00:28:14.966
I feel just as normal as the next person.

00:28:14.966 --> 00:28:18.266
[Dr. Krosnick:] Everyone should know the 
symptoms of diabetes and should have

00:28:18.266 --> 00:28:20.632
a test at least once a year.

00:28:20.633 --> 00:28:23.966
No one can feel that he is exempt from this 
condition.

00:28:23.966 --> 00:28:28.566
[Joan Wolle:] Now you know something about diabetes, the usual symptoms,

00:28:28.566 --> 00:28:30.266
and how to find it early.

00:28:30.266 --> 00:28:34.266
Do you know whether or not you have diabetes?

00:28:34.266 --> 00:28:38.799
[Dr. Lukens:] Don't be like the nervous medical student who thinks he has each disease

00:28:38.800 --> 00:28:46.466
he reads about, but be examined for diabetes at least yearly if you are fair, fat, forty,

00:28:46.466 --> 00:28:49.632
or familied with diabetes.

00:28:49.633 --> 00:28:55.333
If you are not in this ought-to-be-tested group, 
this picture should make you join in

00:28:55.333 --> 00:29:05.799
general detection programs and be a better-informed citizen able to help someone who missed seeing this film.

00:29:05.800 --> 00:29:12.160
[The End]

